Let $\pi$ be a smooth, strictly positive probability density on $\mathbb R^n$. On $C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^n)$ define $$A_\pi=-\Delta-D\log\pi\cdot D.$$ This nonnegative symmetric operator is essentially self-adjoint in $L^2(\pi)$, and $C_c^\infty$ is a core for its closure. In particular, if $f\in L^2(\pi)$ and the distribution $A_\pi f$ belongs to $L^2(\pi)$, then $f\in\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}(A_\pi)$. Its semigroup $P_u=e^{-uA_\pi}$ preserves constants and is realized by the stationary diffusion $$\label{eq:calculus-diffusion}
 dY_u=\sqrt2\,dB_u+D\log\pi(Y_u)\,du,
 \qquad Y_0\sim\pi.$$ For $f\in\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}(A_\pi)$ the stationary Itô formula holds in $L^2$, with drift $-A_\pi f$ and martingale integrand $\sqrt2\,Df$.

For the semigroup of Lemma <a href="#lem:dirichlet-core" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:dirichlet-core">4</a>, if $p_0=q_0\pi$ has finite relative entropy and $p_u=(P_uq_0)\pi$, then, for $0\le a\le b$, $$\label{eq:calculus-stationary-entropy}
 H(p_a\mid\pi)-H(p_b\mid\pi)
 \ge\int_a^b\mathcal I(p_u\mid\pi)\,du.$$ If instead both probability measures evolve by Gaussian convolution, $p_r=p_0*\gamma_r$ and $q_r=q_0*\gamma_r$, then $$\label{eq:calculus-heat-entropy}
 H(p_a\mid q_a)-H(p_b\mid q_b)
 \ge\frac12\int_a^b\mathcal I(p_r\mid q_r)\,dr$$ whenever the entropy on the left at time $a$ is finite. Here $\gamma_r$ is the centered Gaussian with covariance $r\mathrm{Id}$, and time zero is allowed. For the standard Gaussian Ornstein–Uhlenbeck semigroup, Equation <a href="#eq:calculus-stationary-entropy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:calculus-stationary-entropy">[eq:calculus-stationary-entropy]</a> is an equality, including time zero when the initial entropy is finite. Each assertion also holds after integration over conditioning labels whose noises are independent conditional on those labels.

Let $S$ be an $\mathbb R^d$-valued random vector with finite second moment, let $U$ be any side information, and let $G$ be standard Gaussian independent of $(S,U)$. Write $Z_t=\sqrt tS+G$ and $$d(t)=\mathbb E_U H(\mathop{\mathrm{Law}}(Z_t\mid U)\mid\gamma).$$ Then $d(0)=0$, $d(t)\le t\mathbb E|S|^2/2$, and $$\label{eq:calculus-gaussian-signal}
 d(t)=\frac12\int_0^t
 \mathbb E\left|\mathbb E[S\mid Z_v,U]\right|^2dv.$$ The integrand is nondecreasing in $v$. The formula without side information is obtained by taking $U$ constant.

Failure of Theorem <a href="#thm:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:main">1</a> produces a sequence of centered, compactly supported, full dimensional log-concave probabilities $\mu$ such that $$\label{eq:critical-normalization}
 R(\mu)=1,\qquad
 \sup_{|\theta|=1}\mathbb E_\mu e^{\langle X,\theta\rangle^2/a^2}
 \le2,\qquad a\longrightarrow0.$$ For an absolute constant $C$, they also satisfy $$\label{eq:critical-linear-mgf}
 \log\mathbb E_\mu e^{v\cdot X}\le Ca^2|v|^2,
 \qquad \mathop{\mathrm{Cov}}_\mu(X)\le Ca^2\mathrm{Id}.$$

Under Lemma <a href="#lem:optimizer" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:optimizer">10</a>, suppose in addition that $\nu=e^{-V}$ is log-concave with bounded $K=D^2V$. Define $$\eta=f^2\nu,\qquad h=\log f^2,\qquad w=Dh,\qquad
 I=\mathbb E_\eta|w|^2.$$ Then $I=2\lambda H(\eta\mid\nu)>0$, $R(\eta)\le1/\lambda$, each component of $w$ belongs to $\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}(A_\eta)$, and $$\label{eq:critical-score-equation}
 A_\nu h=\lambda h+\tfrac12|w|^2,
 \qquad A_\eta w=(\lambda\mathrm{Id}-K)w.$$ Moreover, $$\label{eq:critical-curvature-mean}
 \mathbb E_\eta\langle w,Kw\rangle\le\lambda|\mathbb E_\eta w|^2.$$

For the sequence in Lemma <a href="#lem:critical-normalization" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:critical-normalization">7</a>, $$\label{eq:critical-R-limit}
 R_s\longrightarrow1\qquad\text{for every }0<s<1/2.$$ At every differentiability point of $R_s$, the fields of Lemma <a href="#lem:critical-two-alternatives" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:critical-two-alternatives">13</a> satisfy the contact identity $$\label{eq:critical-contact-derivative}
 R_s'=R_s\,\frac{\mathbb E_\eta\langle w,K_sw\rangle}{I}.$$ In particular $0\le R_s'\le C_J\sqrt a$ almost everywhere on each fixed compact $J\subset(0,1/2)$.

Consider a sequence of smooth positive probability densities $\eta$ on finite-dimensional Euclidean spaces, with finite second moments. Let $A=-\Delta-D\log\eta\cdot D$, and suppose that $\eta$ satisfies Poincaré with constant at most $\lambda^{-1}$, where $\lambda\to1$. Fix $s=1/8$. Suppose smooth vector fields $b,w$ and a symmetric matrix field $K$ satisfy $$D\log\eta=-\frac{y-b(y)}s+\omega(y),\qquad
 Db=\mathrm{Id}-sK,\qquad 0\le K\le s^{-1}\mathrm{Id},$$ where either $\omega=w$ throughout the sequence or $\omega=0$ throughout the sequence. Assume $b,w\in L^2(\eta)$, $w$ is a gradient, and, with $I=\|w\|_2^2>0$, $$\label{eq:hierarchy-initial}
 w\in\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}A,\qquad
 \|(A-1)w\|_2=o(\sqrt I),\qquad
 |\mathbb E_\eta w|=o(\sqrt I),\qquad
 \langle w,Kw\rangle_2=o(I).$$ Assume also the following prediction and projection properties.*

1.  *For $Y\sim\eta$ and an independent standard Gaussian $G$, set $$U_r=Y+\sqrt r\,G,\quad h_r(u)=\mathbb E[w(Y)\mid U_r=u],\quad
     m(r)=I^{-1}\mathbb E|h_r(U_r)|^2.$$ For every fixed $0<r<1/4$, one has $m(r)\to e^{-r}$.*



2.  *For every fixed $c<\infty$, $$\label{eq:hierarchy-input-projection}
     \sup_{\substack{\Pi^2=\Pi=\Pi^*\ \mathrm{deterministic}\\
                       \mathop{\mathrm{rank}}\Pi\le cI}}
           \|\Pi w\|_2^2=o(I).$$ For every $\epsilon>0$ there is a deterministic projection $\Pi_\epsilon$ of rank at most $C_\epsilon I$ such that $$\label{eq:hierarchy-input-outliers}
     \left\|\mathbb E[(1-\Pi_\epsilon)bb^*(1-\Pi_\epsilon)]\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}
     +\left\|\mathbb E[(1-\Pi_\epsilon)\omega\omega^*
                      (1-\Pi_\epsilon)]\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le\epsilon$$ eventually along the sequence.*


3.  *If $\omega=w$, the conditional covariance $C_r=\mathop{\mathrm{Cov}}(w(Y)\mid U_r)$ satisfies, for every fixed $r\in(0,1/4)$ and $a>0$, $$\label{eq:hierarchy-input-tail}
     \mathbb E\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}\bigl(C_r\mathbf 1_{\{C_r>a\}}\bigr)=o(I).$$ No conditional covariance-tail assumption is made when $\omega=0$.*




*Then there are totally symmetric tensor fields $T_l$ of order $l+1$, for every fixed integer $l\ge0$, with $T_0=w$, such that $$\label{eq:hierarchy-eigen}
 \begin{gathered}
 \|T_l\|_2^2=(1+o(1))I,\qquad
 \|(A-1)T_l\|_2=o(\sqrt I),\\
 \|DT_l-T_{l+1}\|_2=o(\sqrt I).
 \end{gathered}$$ $$\label{eq:hierarchy-curvature}
 \langle T_l,K_1T_l\rangle_2=o(I).$$ $$\label{eq:hierarchy-projection}
 \sup_{\substack{\Pi^2=\Pi=\Pi^*\ \mathrm{deterministic}\\
                   \mathop{\mathrm{rank}}\Pi\le cI}}
      \|\Pi_1T_l\|_2^2=o(I)
 \qquad\text{for every fixed }c<\infty.$$ Every $T_l$ belongs componentwise to $\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}A$. For $l\ge1$ it can be chosen in $\bigcap_{p\ge1}\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}A^p$. By symmetry, the specified slot in Equations <a href="#eq:hierarchy-curvature" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:hierarchy-curvature">[eq:hierarchy-curvature]</a> and <a href="#eq:hierarchy-projection" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:hierarchy-projection">[eq:hierarchy-projection]</a> may be any slot. All assertions can be made simultaneous for any fixed finite collection of levels and parameters.

For the data of Proposition <a href="#prop:hierarchy" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:hierarchy">20</a>, define $g_r(y)=\mathbb E_G h_r(y+\sqrt r\,G)$. For each $r>0$, $$\label{eq:hierarchy-bessel}
 \langle w,g_r\rangle_2=m(r)I,\qquad
 \sum_{j\ge0}\frac{r^j}{j!}\|D^jg_r\|_2^2\le m(r)I.$$ In particular, every fixed derivative appearing here is in the form domain of $A$.

If $F$ is a square-integrable random variable with values in a finite-dimensional Hilbert space and $Z$ is a centered Euclidean random vector with $\mathop{\mathrm{Cov}}Z\le c\mathrm{Id}$, then $$\label{eq:hierarchy-crosscov}
 |\mathbb E(F\otimes Z)|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2\le c\mathbb E|F|^2.$$ Next, let $A$ be a sequence of nonnegative Dirichlet operators with kernel the constants and spectral gap at least $\lambda\to1$. If fields $S$ in their form domains satisfy $$|\mathbb ES|^2=o(I),\qquad \|S\|_2^2=(1+o(1))I,\qquad
 \|DS\|_2^2\le(1+o(1))I,$$ then $\widetilde S=e^{1-A}S$ satisfies $$\label{eq:hierarchy-smoothing}
 \|\widetilde S-S\|_2+\|D(\widetilde S-S)\|_2=o(\sqrt I),
 \qquad \|(A-1)\widetilde S\|_2=o(\sqrt I).$$

Let symmetric fields $T_l$ satisfy Equation <a href="#eq:hierarchy-eigen" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:hierarchy-eigen">[eq:hierarchy-eigen]</a>. For fixed integers $l,m\ge0$ and fixed $t>0$, set $N=B_t/\sqrt t$. Then $$\label{eq:hierarchy-diffusion-expansion}
 T_l(Y_t)=e^{-t}\sum_{j=0}^m\frac{(2t)^{j/2}}{j!}
       T_{l+j}(Y_0):\mathfrak h_j(N)+\mathcal E_{l,m,t},$$ where the colon contracts the last $j$ tensor slots and $$\label{eq:hierarchy-diffusion-remainder}
 \limsup\frac{\|\mathcal E_{l,m,t}\|_2}{\sqrt I}
 \le\frac{(2t)^{(m+1)/2}}{\sqrt{(m+1)!}}.$$ For $0\le t\le T<\infty$ the same bound holds uniformly with $t$ replaced on the right by $T$ and with the expansion at $t=0$ interpreted by continuity. The limits are also simultaneous for any fixed finite set of starting levels. In particular the normalized remainder tends to zero when the sequence limit is followed by $m\to\infty$.

Let $\eta$ be a smooth positive probability density, let $A=-\Delta-D\log\eta\cdot D$, and put $H=D^2(-\log\eta)$. Let $P=(P_{i\alpha})$ have one vector slot $i$ and a finite-dimensional coefficient index $\alpha$. Suppose every coefficient belongs to $\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}A$ and $HP\in L^2(\eta)$. Then the weighted adjoint divergence $$(D^*P)_\alpha=-\sum_i\partial_iP_{i\alpha}
                   -\sum_i(\partial_i\log\eta)P_{i\alpha}$$ belongs to $L^2(\eta)$ and satisfies $$\label{eq:hierarchy-divergence-bound}
 \|D^*P\|_2^2\le\|DP\|_2^2+\langle P,HP\rangle_2.$$ For every coefficient field $F\in\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}A$, $$\label{eq:hierarchy-differentiated-operator}
 \langle AF,D^*P\rangle_2
 =\langle DF,AP\rangle_2+\langle DF,HP\rangle_2.$$ In particular this identity requires $HP\in L^2$, not $HDF\in L^2$.

Let $\psi$ be a finite direct sum of symmetric tensors, each with at least $k\ge2$ slots. Let $\rho$ be its one-slot reduction and $R=\sqrt\rho$. For a self-adjoint matrix $H$, let $H_{\mathrm{av}}$ act on each component by averaging the action of $H$ over its slots. Then $$\label{eq:matrix-copied-slots}
 \|H_{\mathrm{av}}\psi\|^2
 \le\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(RHRH)+\frac{2}{k}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(\rho H^2).$$

There are measurable symmetric matrix fields $H_i$, $1\le i\le n$, solving $$\label{eq:matrix-sylvester}
 D_iR=\frac{H_iR+RH_i}{2},$$ with zero kernel–kernel block, such that $$\label{eq:matrix-saturation}
 \|DR\|_2^2\longrightarrow1,\qquad
 \sum_i\mathbb E\|[H_i,R]\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2=o(1),\qquad
 \sum_i\mathbb E\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(R^2H_i^2)\longrightarrow1.$$ Moreover, $$\label{eq:matrix-action-errors}
 \sum_i\left(\|D_iR-H_iR\|_2^2+
 \|D_i\Psi-(H_i)_{(1)}\Psi\|_2^2\right)=o(1).$$ The first slot can be replaced by any existing slot in each component.

The matrices in Lemma <a href="#lem:matrix-saturation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:matrix-saturation">29</a> satisfy $$\label{eq:matrix-balance}
 \mathbb E\left\|\sum_iH_i\rho H_i-\rho\right\|_1=o(1),\qquad
 \mathbb E\left\|R\left(\sum_iH_i^2\right)R-\rho\right\|_1=o(1).$$ They can be replaced by symmetric matrix fields, still denoted $H_i$, so that Equation <a href="#eq:matrix-action-errors" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:matrix-action-errors">[eq:matrix-action-errors]</a>, the commutator conclusion of Equation <a href="#eq:matrix-saturation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:matrix-saturation">[eq:matrix-saturation]</a>, and Equation <a href="#eq:matrix-balance" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:matrix-balance">[eq:matrix-balance]</a> hold, and $$\label{eq:matrix-column-bound}
 S:=\sum_iH_i^2\le4\mathrm{Id},\qquad
 \mathbb E\|(S-1)R\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2=o(1).$$ The total change satisfies $\sum_i\mathbb E\|(H_i^{\mathrm{new}}-H_i^{\mathrm{old}})R\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2=o(1)$.

For the bounded columns just constructed, $$\label{eq:matrix-index-exchange}
 \sum_{i,j}\mathbb E\|[H_i,H_j]R\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2=o(1),\qquad
 \mathbb E\sum_{i,a,b}|(H_iR)_{ab}-(H_aR)_{ib}|^2=o(1).$$ These conclusions also hold after a symmetric perturbation satisfying Equation <a href="#eq:matrix-weighted-change" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:matrix-weighted-change">[eq:matrix-weighted-change]</a>, provided the new column has a uniform operator bound.

After enlarging the initial probability space by an independent uniform variable, there are a positive block matrix $R_*=\bigoplus_\beta\beta\mathrm{Id}_\beta$, with zero on $\ker R$, and symmetric matrices $H_i$ supported on entries whose row, column, and outer index belong to the same positive block, with the following properties. Their total change satisfies Equation <a href="#eq:matrix-weighted-change" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:matrix-weighted-change">[eq:matrix-weighted-change]</a>, $R_*\le R\le(1+\epsilon)R_*$ on the positive support for $\epsilon\to0$, and the column bound $S\le4\mathrm{Id}$ holds. With the positive weighted trace $$\label{eq:matrix-weighted-trace}
 \tau(U)=\mathbb E\sum_\beta\beta^2\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\beta U_\beta,$$ where the expectation also includes any fresh noises present, we have $\tau(1)\to1$ and $$\label{eq:matrix-tracial-defects}
 \tau((S-1)^2)=o(1),\quad
 \sum_{i,j}\tau(|[H_i,H_j]|^2)=o(1),\quad
 \mathbb E\sum_\beta\beta^2\sum_{i,a,b\in\beta}
 |(H_i)_{ab}-(H_a)_{ib}|^2=o(1).$$ For a vector $v$ in a positive block define $H[v]=\sum_{i\in\beta}v_iH_i$. We can further impose, pointwise, $$\label{eq:flattening-bound}
 \|H[v]\|_{\mathrm{HS}}\le2|v|.$$ The bound uses ordinary, unnormalized Hilbert–Schmidt norm. All the derivative-action and balance conclusions above continue to hold for these matrices in the initial coordinates.

Let the block matrices and $\tau$ be those of Lemma <a href="#lem:matrix-blocks" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:matrix-blocks">32</a>. For independent standard Gaussian vectors $N_1,\ldots,N_q$, independent of the initial data, put $D_r=H[N_r]$. Then, for every fixed word $r_1,\ldots,r_m$, $$\label{eq:matrix-gaussian-joint-law}
 \tau(D_{r_1}\cdots D_{r_m})
 \longrightarrow \mathbb E(Z_{r_1}\cdots Z_{r_m}),$$ where $Z_1,\ldots,Z_q$ are independent scalar standard normals. Products of any fixed length can be reordered with error tending to zero in $L^2(\tau)$. For every integer $p\ge1$ and real $u\ge0$, $$\label{eq:matrix-gaussian-moment-bound}
 \mathbb E_{N_r}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\beta D_r^{2p}
 \le4^p(2p-1)!!\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\beta1,
 \qquad
 \tau(e^{u|D_r|})\le2e^{2u^2}\tau(1).$$ If $\mathfrak h_j$ is the tensor Hermite polynomial of Lemma <a href="#lem:hermite-expansion" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:hermite-expansion">24</a> and $\mathfrak h_j^{(1)}$ is its one-dimensional version, then for each fixed $j$, $$\label{eq:matrix-wick-reduction}
 \tau\left(\left|
 \sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_j}H_{i_j}\cdots H_{i_1}
      \mathfrak h_j(N_1)_{i_1\ldots i_j}
 -\mathfrak h_j^{(1)}(D_1)\right|^2\right)=o(1).$$

Let $Y_u$ be the stationary diffusion with invariant law $\eta$ and driving Brownian motion $B_u$, and fix $t>0$. Make the matrix construction of Lemma <a href="#lem:matrix-blocks" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:matrix-blocks">32</a> at $Y_0$, using an independent random block offset. In its orthonormal frame put $N=B_t/\sqrt t$; conditionally on the initial data and block offset, $N$ is a standard Gaussian vector. Define, in the initial spatial coordinates, $$\label{eq:matrix-frozen-covariance}
 \sigma=\bigoplus_\beta\beta^2
       \exp\bigl(\sqrt{8t}\,H[N]-4t\mathrm{Id}_\beta\bigr),$$ and set $\sigma=0$ on the omitted kernel. Then $$\label{eq:matrix-frozen-law}
 \mathbb E\|R(Y_t)-\sigma^{1/2}\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2\longrightarrow0.$$ The matrices defining $\sigma$ obey the bounded-column and input-map bounds <a href="#eq:matrix-column-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:matrix-column-bound">[eq:matrix-column-bound]</a> and <a href="#eq:flattening-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:flattening-bound">[eq:flattening-bound]</a>, the weighted defects <a href="#eq:matrix-tracial-defects" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:matrix-tracial-defects">[eq:matrix-tracial-defects]</a>, and the mixed moment law of Lemma <a href="#lem:matrix-gaussian-moments" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:matrix-gaussian-moments">33</a>.

For $v,w\ge0$, not both zero, put $$\label{eq:obs-multiplier}
 M_L(v,w)=(v+w)^2\int_0^\infty
 \frac{dT}{
 (1+Tv^2/L)(1+Tw^2/L)
 (1+(1+1/L)Tv^2)(1+(1+1/L)Tw^2)}.$$ Set $M_L(0,0)=0$. Then $$\label{eq:obs-multiplier-bounds}
 M_L(v,w)\le4\log(1+L)
 \quad\hbox{for all }v,w\ge0,
 \qquad
 M_L(v,w)\le9
 \quad\hbox{if }\tfrac12\le v/w\le2.$$ The second bound concerns positive $v,w$ and is independent of $L$.

For $T>0$ let $h_T(v)=\sqrt{1+f_L(Tv^2)}$, $v\ge0$, and put $$K_L=\int_0^\infty
 \frac{dq}{(1+q/L)^3(1+(1+1/L)q)}.$$ Then $0<K_L\le\log(1+L)$, and for arbitrary positive semidefinite matrices $A,B$ of the same size, $$\label{eq:obs-matrix-stability}
 \int_0^\infty\|h_T(A)-h_T(B)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2\,\frac{dT}{T^2}
 \le K_L\|A-B\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2.$$ Let $\sigma$ be the frozen covariance in Proposition <a href="#prop:frozen-law" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:frozen-law">34</a>, and couple $$\widetilde Z_T=(\mathrm{Id}+f_L(T\sigma))^{1/2}G$$ to $Z_T$ using the same independent Gaussian. For every fixed $t,L$, $$\label{eq:obs-observation-stability}
 \int_0^\infty\mathbb E|Z_T-\widetilde Z_T|^2\,\frac{dT}{T^2}=o(1).$$

Let $N$ be a standard Gaussian vector conditional on $\mathcal D$, and let $Z$ be any observation for which $\mathsf I(N;Z\mid\mathcal D)<\infty$. With independent standard Gaussian $G'$ set $$N_u=\alpha N+\sqrt{1-\alpha^2}\,G',\qquad \alpha=e^{-u},\quad u>0.$$ The relative score of its posterior, with respect to the standard Gaussian marginal, is $$\label{eq:obs-ou-score}
 D\log\frac{p_{N_u\mid Z,\mathcal D}}{\gamma}(N_u)
 =\frac{\alpha}{1-\alpha^2}
   \mathbb E[N-\alpha N_u\mid N_u,Z,\mathcal D].$$ For every compact interval $\mathcal J\subset(0,\infty)$, $$\label{eq:obs-ou-information}
 \mathsf I(N;Z\mid\mathcal D)
 \ge\int_{\mathcal J}\frac{\alpha^2}{(1-\alpha^2)^2}
 \mathbb E\left|\mathbb E[N-\alpha N_u\mid N_u,Z,\mathcal D]\right|^2\,du.$$ For the covariance observation in Equation <a href="#eq:obs-covariance-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:obs-covariance-definition">[eq:obs-covariance-definition]</a>, $$\label{eq:obs-data-processing}
 \mathsf I(Y_t;Z_T)\ge\mathsf I(N;Z_T\mid\mathcal D).$$

Fix compact intervals $\mathcal J,\mathcal X\subset(0,\infty)$, and write $\kappa(u)=\alpha^2/(1-\alpha^2)^2$, where $\alpha=e^{-u}$. Let $P(u,x,a,b)$ be a real polynomial, evaluated on matrices with any one fixed ordering of each monomial. On each positive block define $$A_\beta=H_\beta[N_\beta],\qquad
 A_{u,\beta}=H_\beta[(N_u)_\beta],\qquad
 B_{\beta,x}=H_\beta[(Z_{x/\beta^2})_\beta],$$ and $P_{\beta,u,x}=P(u,x,A_{u,\beta},B_{\beta,x})$. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 \int_0^\infty\mathsf I(Y_t;Z_T)\,\frac{dT}{T^2}
 \ge{}&\int_{\mathcal J}\int_{\mathcal X}\kappa(u)
 \mathbb E\sum_\beta\beta^2\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_\beta
 \bigl(2(A_\beta-\alpha A_{u,\beta})P_{\beta,u,x}
 \notag\\[-2pt]
 &\hspace{43mm}-C_1P_{\beta,u,x}^*P_{\beta,u,x}\bigr)
 \,\frac{dx}{x^2}\,du.
 \label{eq:obs-block-test-bound}
\end{aligned}$$ Here the constant $C_1$ is the absolute constant in Equation <a href="#eq:obs-block-bounds" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:obs-block-bounds">[eq:obs-block-bounds]</a>.

For every fixed $t>0$ and $L\ge1$, let $g_x$ be defined by Equation <a href="#eq:obs-g-function" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:obs-g-function">[eq:obs-g-function]</a>, and let $n,g$ be independent scalar standard Gaussians. Then $$\label{eq:obs-information-lower}
 \liminf\int_0^\infty\mathsf I(Y_t;Z_T)\,\frac{dT}{T^2}
 \ge C_1^{-1}\int_0^\infty
       \mathsf I\big(n;g_x(n)g\big)\,\frac{dx}{x^2}.$$ The constant $C_1$ is universal and is independent of $t,L$.

Let $n,g$ be independent scalar standard Gaussians and, for $t\ge1$, define $$\ell=\exp(\sqrt{8t}\,n-4t),\qquad
 z_x=\sqrt{1+f_t(x\ell)}\,g.$$ There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $h_0\ge1$ such that, for all $t\ge h_0$, $$\label{eq:obs-scalar-divergence}
 \int_0^\infty\mathsf I(n;z_x)\,\frac{dx}{x^2}
 \ge c\mathbb E\big[\ell\mathbf 1_{\{\ell\ge e^{2t}\}}\big]
                  \log(t/h_0)
 \ge\frac c2\log(t/h_0).$$
